; CLIFF 1.0 — Contextual Localization Integrated File Format
; Normative ABNF grammar (RFC 5234; case-sensitive string literals per RFC 7405).
;
; LEXICAL NOTE
; ------------
; The grammar below operates after a lexical pre-pass that:
;   * decodes UTF-8 (an optional leading U+FEFF BOM is removed),
;   * normalizes CRLF to LF (a bare CR is invalid),
;   * appends a final LF when the stream does not end with one, so that every
;     line production below is LF-terminated,
;   * removes blank lines (lines containing only SP / HTAB),
;   * removes comment lines (optional SP / HTAB followed by "#").
; Comments and blank lines may therefore appear between any two grammar
; productions, but never inside a quoted string. Their removal is transparent
; everywhere except one place: a blank or comment line terminates the
; continuation of the preceding field, so a parser MUST record that boundary
; before discarding the line (see the semantic constraints below).
;
; This is the TOLERANT grammar: surrounding whitespace and both ":" and "="
; are accepted so the format is pleasant to read and type by hand. The
; CANONICAL form is fixed in spec §Serialization: "key: value" with exactly
; one space after the colon, section and entry lines at column 0 with no
; trailing whitespace.
;
; All non-ASCII Unicode scalar values are permitted in strings and comments.
; They are not enumerated here because RFC 5234 char-val ranges are
; US-ASCII-oriented; parsers apply the restrictions in spec §Lexical
; Structure.

cliff-file            = version-line *header-line *section
version-line         = *WSP %s"CLIFF" SP %s"1.0" *WSP LF

header-line          = field-line
section              = section-line *group-metadata-line *entry

section-line         = *WSP "[" group-path "]" *WSP LF
group-path           = name *( "." name )

group-metadata-line  = field-line
entry                = entry-line *field-line
entry-line           = *WSP "<" name ">" *WSP LF
                      ; single-line marker; no closing tag exists, and < > in
                      ; strings remain ordinary payload characters.

field-line           = *WSP field *WSP LF *continuation-line
continuation-line    = *WSP ( string-value / list ) *WSP LF
                      ; attaches to the immediately preceding field: string
                      ; fragments only after scalar string fields, list
                      ; fragments only after list fields.
field                = key *WSP ( "=" / ":" ) *WSP value
value                = string-value / list / integer / name / language-tag
string-value         = string *( *WSP string )
                      ; adjacent strings concatenate verbatim (C rules) and
                      ; are allowed on any scalar string field.
list                 = "[" *WSP [ list-items ] *WSP "]"
list-items           = list-item *( *WSP "," *WSP list-item ) *WSP [ "," ]
list-item            = string / integer / name / language-tag
                      ; a list is single-line and never nests another list.

key                  = name

; Identifiers: lowercase kebab-case only. No uppercase letters, no
; underscores, no dots. Fixed tags (type/emotion/status/variant) are also
; names, which is why every fixed tag is a lowercase word.
name                 = %x61-7A *( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "-" )

integer              = 1*DIGIT

; BCP 47 language tag. The full BCP 47 grammar is defined by RFC 5646 and is
; normative; this production is a permissive envelope for ABNF completeness.
; Comparison is case-insensitive per BCP 47.
language-tag         = ALPHA *( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" )

string               = double-string / single-string
double-string        = DQUOTE *( double-char / double-escape ) DQUOTE
double-char          = %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7E / non-ascii
                      ; excludes DQUOTE (%x22), backslash (%x5C), controls.
double-escape        = "\" ( DQUOTE / "\" / "n" / "r" / "t" )
single-string        = SQUOTE *( single-char / single-escape ) SQUOTE
single-char          = %x20-26 / %x28-5B / %x5D-7E / non-ascii
                      ; excludes SQUOTE (%x27), backslash (%x5C), controls.
single-escape        = "\" ( SQUOTE / "\" / "n" / "r" / "t" )
non-ascii            = %x80-F4
                      ; practical envelope; parsers MUST validate UTF-8
                      ; scalar values per the specification.

WSP                  = SP / HTAB
SP                   = %x20
HTAB                 = %x09
DQUOTE               = %x22
SQUOTE               = %x27
LF                   = %x0A
ALPHA                = %x41-5A / %x61-7A
DIGIT                = %x30-39

; Semantic constraints (enforced by validators, not by ABNF):
;  * version-line must be the first non-ignored line and must be "CLIFF 1.0".
;  * Header fields occur before the first section-line; group metadata lines
;    occur only before the first entry-line of a section.
;  * namespace, clan, source-language, and target-language are all required.
;    The layout (folder "<target-language>/<clan>.cliff" or flat
;    "<clan>.<target-language>.cliff") is a delivery convention that MUST
;    agree with the header; it never supplies missing header values.
;  * Entry-level: source, type (direct or inherited), and status are
;    required; target is optional and required only for status translated /
;    reviewed / final (status initial allows a missing target).
;  * Section paths and entry ids are unique; duplicate entry ids are a hard
;    error (never last-wins); canonical IDs
;    namespace.clan.group-path.entry-id are unique and lowercase kebab-case.
;  * Fixed tag vocabularies: type, emotion, status, variant. Status tags are
;    initial, translated, reviewed, final (XLIFF 2.x state model).
;  * A field separator is the first "=" or ":" outside a quoted string; the
;    entry anchor is the single-line marker "<id>". "<" and ">" have no other
;    structural use and never pair across lines.
;  * Every key appears at most once per scope (header, group, entry);
;    duplicate keys are validity errors — there are no repeatable fields.
;  * Strings containing braces must have balanced braces; the check applies
;    to the fully concatenated value after continuation lines are joined.
;  * List-typed fields (emotion, dependency, reference) are always written
;    as a list, even for a single item; a bare scalar in a list-typed field
;    is a validity error. dependency and reference list items are quoted
;    path/string values.
;  * Continuation lines (string fragments after scalar string fields, bare
;    lists after list fields) concatenate verbatim / extend the list in
;    order. A blank or comment line ends a continuation; an orphan
;    continuation line is a syntax error. Lists themselves never span lines
;    and list items never continue across lines.
